>[!def] Efficiency
>Let $x$ be a rv with finite alphabet of size $M$, we call $\log_2 M$ the **nominal information**, then we can define the efficiency as:
>$$\eta_x=\frac{H(x)}{\log_2M}$$
>Notice that if we talk about bits $M=2\implies\log_2M=1$





# 3) Quantisation

This chapter is the recap of the first part of chapter 3 of the book

The aim of this course is to learn how to transmit data. Data is usually found in **analog** form (ex. voice) and it is possible to transmit such data using analog modulation. The term analog means "analogo", similar. This means that an analog modulation changes the signal according to a specific criteria

On the other hand **digital** signals are sequence of digits, hence the name. This chapter will explain how to **convert analog to digital**.

One of the main components of the Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) is the quantiser, as shown in the figure
![[Pasted image 20241004143649.png|ADC|450]]
The first step of an ADC is the **sampling step**. This step is fairly easy by following [[#^74f625]] and most importantly can be **reconstructed**. This steps **discretises the time** thus $x(t) \rightarrow x[nT_s]$.

The second step is the **quantiser**, this step **discretises the values of the signal**, thus $Q:\mathbb{R} = \{\mathbb{Q}_0,...,\mathbb{Q}_{L-a}\}$
A quantiser can be defined using two representations:
![[Pasted image 20241004145453.png|Two equivalent representations of a quantiser|350]]
>[!col]
>The first representation shows how a quantiser is essentially a "map". For commodity we set the number of levels as a power of 2 $L=2^b$ because a bitmap will be used (Signed Magnitude).
>$$ $$
>The second representation is more effective as showing the error as a kind of noise
>
>![[Pasted image 20241004150820.png|Uniform Quantiser|350]]

>[!def] Uniform Quantiser
>The **quantisation threshold** are the values $v_i$ with $i \in 0,...,L$ where each step of the quantiser takes place
>If they are equally spaced the quantiser has a **uniform quantisation step size** as $\Delta = v_i-v_{i-1} \\ \forall i = 2,...,L-1$

>[!def] Mid-Riser Representation
>The representation in the picture is called **mid-riser** as 0 doesn't exist and get's rounded to decimal 1 as there are $L/2$ positive and negative values.

By looking at the second representation it is clear that the error is given as:
$$a(nT_s)+e_q(nT_s)=a_q(nT_s)\implies e_q(nT_s) = a_q(nT_s) - a(nT_s)$$

>[!def] Saturation Error and Granular Noise Error
>The area between the values $v_1$ until $v_L$ is called **granular noise region** as the quantiser approximates the values with a maximum error of  $-\Delta/2\leq e_q \leq\Delta/2$.
>
>Outside of this area there are the **saturation** values, which get rounded with an error bigger than $\Delta/2$
>
>More formally we define the (upper) **saturation value** as $v_{sat}=v_{L-1}+\Delta$
>![[Pasted image 20241004153545.png|Graphical representation with L=8|350]]

^47e80f

A quick recap of these values and their relationships is:
$$\begin{gather}
L=2^b \\
L\Delta=2v_{sat} \\
\text{that is} \\
2^{b-1}\Delta=v_{sat}
\end{gather}$$
## 3.1) Quantisation Error Analysis
This analysis will be done as a probabilistic analysis as the additive error $e_q(nT_s)$ will be considered as a random variable. It could seem strange as the error is perfectly defined 
 from $e_q(nT_s) = a_q(nT_s) - a(nT_s)$. But since it is a really complex value to obtain it can be approximated as [randomness doesn't exist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randomness). The main result of this chapter will be the following definition/theorem.

>[!theorem] Signal-to-Noise Rattio (SNR)
>We define the **SNR** as: 
>$$\Lambda_q=\frac{M_a}{M_{e_q}}=\frac{E[a^2(nT_s)]}{E[e_q^2(nT_s)]}$$
>Since it is a fraction of powers we can express it in dB:
>$$\text{SNR}\ [dB]=10\log_{10}\Lambda_q \ [dB]$$
>If $P_{sat}<<1$ we can use the following formula
>$$\boxed{\text{SNR}=10\log_{10} 3+10\log_{10} L^2 +10\log_{10}k_f^2=4.77+6,02b+20\log_{10}\frac{\sigma_a}{v_\text{sat}} \ [dB]}$$
>Otherwise it can be obtained via the computation of statistical powers directly

We start by assuming the following properties of the signal $e_q(nT_s)$:
1) $e_q(nT_s)$ is a rv
2) $e_q(nT_s)$ is uncorrelated with $a(nT_s)$: $E[a(nT_s)e_q(nT_s)]=0$
3) $e_q(nT_s)$ is uncorrelated with itself (it is a white noise): $E[e_q(nT_s)e_q(nT_s)]=0$
4) $e_q(nT_s)$ is uniformly distributed in $[-\Delta/2,\Delta/2]$:
$$\boxed{p_{sat}=\frac{1}{\Delta}\text{rect}(\frac{a}{\Delta})}$$
Recall what $P_{sat}$ is:
$$\boxed{P_{sat}(a)=P[\mid a(nT_s)\mid>v_{sat}]}$$
Our **working assumption** is that $P_{sat}<<1$ so that $e_{sat}\simeq0$

Moreover $a(nT_s)$ and $e_q(nT_s)$ have the seam mean (usually = 0) and thus we can't compare them. We can use the **statistical power** ($E[x^2]$, that is the variance with $E[x]=0$ )to find some useful data.



From 4) we can find An approximation of $M_{e_{q}}$:
$$M_{e_q}=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}a^2p_{e_q}(a)da=\frac{1}{\Delta}\int_{-\Delta/2}^{\Delta/2}a^2da=\frac{\Delta^2}{12}$$
<span style="color:rgb(125, 125, 125)">(By recalling that $var(x)=\int x^2f_x(x)dx - E(x)^2$)</span> 

Then we have:
$$\Lambda_q=\frac{\sigma_a^2}{\Delta^2/12}$$
By recalling that $\Delta=v_{sat}/2^{b-1}$
$$\Lambda_q=\frac{12\sigma^2_a\cdot2^{2(b-1)}}{v^s_{sat}}=3k_f^2L^2$$
By defining the **loading factor** $k_f=\frac{\sigma_a}{v_\text{sat}}$ an recalling $L=2^b$
In dB this is:
$$\boxed{\text{SNR}=10\log_{10} 3+10\log_{10} L^2 +10\log_{10}k_f^2=4.77+6,02b+20\log_{10}\frac{\sigma_a}{v_{\text{sat}}} \ [dB]}$$
>[!rmk|*] Pre-processing of a signal
>Given our assumption a signal needs to have the seam mean as the central value $v_{sat}/s$ of the quantiser. Moreover the signal needs to be normalised in amplitude.
>Fortunately it is really easy to do so:
>Let $a(t)$ be the signal to send to the quantiser. By defining a signal of type 
>$$b(t)=\frac1Aa(t)+c$$
>there is no loss of generality and the quantiser hold for this signal

>[!rmk|*]  $M_{e_q}=(1-P_{sat})M_{e_{gr}}+P_{sat}M_{e_{sat}}$
>If the working assumption doesn't hold we must use this property. Recall that:
>$$p_{e_{gr}}=\frac{1}{\Delta}\text{rect}(\frac{a}{\Delta})\implies M_{e_{gr}}=\int_{-v_{sat}}^{v_{sat}}[Q(u)-u]^2p_a(u)du=\boxed{\frac{\Delta^2}{12}}$$
>$$M_{e_{sat}}=\int_{-\infty}^{-v_{sat}}[Q_0-u]^2p_a(u)du+\int_{v_{sat}}^{\infty}[Q_{L-1}-u]^2p_a(u)du$$
>Usually $Q_{L-1}-u=v_{sat}-\frac{\Delta}{2}-t$
>

It is important to notice that **adding one bit reduces the noise to 1/4**.
Add 1 to b $\implies$ L doubles $\implies$ $\Lambda_q$ quadruples $\implies$ 4x is roughly +6 dB

>[!rmk|*]
>If the signal is uniformly distributed and the quantizer has correct $v_{sat}$, then $4,77=-20\log{k_f}$

# 4) Characterisation Of Transmission Media And Devices

>[!col]
>The easiest way to transmit information is through a **two port network**. The easiest one is represented in the picture. In the frequency domain we have:$$\begin{gather}
V_L(f)=V_i(f)G(f), \ G(f)=\frac{Z_L(f)}{Z_S(f)+Z_L(f)} \\
\mathcal P_{v_L}(f)=\mathcal P_{v_i}(f)|G(f)|^2
\end{gather}$$
>
>
>![[Pasted image 20241030095259.png|Two Terminal Network|250]]
#### Electrical Power
The **average power transferred to $Z_L$due to $v_i$** is:
$$P_v=\lim_{u\rightarrow+\infty}\frac1{2u}\int_{-u}^{+u}v_L(t)i_L(t)dt$$
The term in the integral is the instantaneous power.

It can also be a **statistical measure**, if $v_L$ and $i_L$ are disjointly wss and ergodic rps
$$P_v=E[v_L(i)i_L(i)]$$
By exploiting the cross correlation and cross spectral power
$$P_v=r_{v_L}i_L(0)=\int\mathcal P_{v_Li_L}(f)df$$
Since they are both real signals the Ftf is Hermitian and thus the odd imaginary part doesn't contribute
$$P_v=\int\Re[\mathcal P_{v_Li_L}(f)]df$$
and the expression in the integral is the **power density**


From Ohm's law and the obtained results we can derive that:
$$\begin{align}
&p_v(f)=P_{v_i}(f)\frac{R_L(f)}{|Z_S(f)+Z_L(f)|^2}\\
&p_v(f)=P_{v_L}(f)\frac{R_L(f)}{|Z_L(f)|^2}\\
\end{align}$$

#### Load Matching and Available Power
In a system with given $\mathcal P_{v_i}, Z_S$ what is the right $Z_L$ to choose in order to minimise power loss?

>[!thm] Load Matching
>The maximum transfer of electrical power is given under the condition
>$$Z_L(f)=\overline{Z_S(f)}$$

>[!rmk|*] Narrowband Power
>If $Z_S$ is constant in the band of $v_i$ we have that
>$$P_v=M_{v_L}\frac{R_L}{|Z_L|^2}$$


#### 4.2) Thermal Noise
>[!col]
>In any conductor the electrons move due to thermal agitation. This movement is usually negligible, but with an accurate enough voltmeter it is possible to measure some currents. This is called **thermal noise** and adds additively to the regular currents due to superimposition. 
>The noise can be seen as a rv with zero mean but we usually consider a small value of the current/voltage over time and in this timeframe the mean isn't guaranteed to be 0.
>
>![[Pasted image 20241030102946.png|Noisy Circuit and Eqivalent Noise Source|250]]

The **power spectral density** of the noise in a resistor is modeled as a WSS rp with PSD:
$$P_{w_i}(f)=2kTR\gamma(f)$$
So at room temperature $T=T_0=290[K]$ and with $f<<10^{11}$ we have that $\gamma(f)=1$
So at the same temperature and under *load matching* the power density is
$$p_w(f)=\frac12kT\gamma(f)$$

>[!def] Noise Temperature
>In a noisy circuit with $w_i$ the noise voltage and power density $p_w(f)$ at the load due to $w_i$, we define the **noise temperature** of the device as
>$$T(f)=\frac{p_w(f)}{k/2}$$


## 4.2) Two-Port Model

>[!col]
>In a real world scenario a two port model isn't ideal anymore and thus we must consider a different power going in the network and coming out from it. The **transfer function on the output** is $G_2(f)=\frac{V_2}{V_1}$ and $G_{ch}(f)=\frac{V_L}{V_1}=G_2G_L$
>
>
>![[Pasted image 20241030105016.png|Two-Port Model|350]]

>[!def] Network Power Gain
>With powers densities it is possible to evaluate average powers, thus we define a real valued, non-negative function that relates the densities at the input and output of the network:
>$$g(f)=\frac{p_{v,out}(f)}{p_{v,in}(f)}$$
>It is usually expressed in [dB]
>Moreover if $g(f)<1$ we speak of attenuation $a(f)=1/g(f)$

In a matched condition $Z_1=\overline{Z_S}, \ Z_L=\overline{Z_2}$ we have
$$\begin{gather}
&&p_{v,in}=\mathcal P_{v_1}\frac{R_1}{|Z_1|^2} \\
&&p_{v,out}=\mathcal P_{v_L}\frac{R_L}{|Z_L|^2} \\
&&\mathcal P{v_L}=\mathcal P_{v_1}|G_{ch}|^2 \\
\end{gather}$$
from here, if $Z_1=Z_2$ we have that $g(f)=|G_{ch}|^2$

#### Noisy 2-Port Network

Since the two power sources (noise, regular) are uncorrelated we have that
$$\begin{gather}
p_{w,out}^S(f)=p_{w,in}^S(f)g(f)=\frac12kT_s(f)g(f) \\
p_{w,out}=p_{w,out}^S+p_{w,out}^A
\end{gather}$$
then the **noise temperature of a matched two-port network** is
$$T_A(f)=\frac{p_{w,out}^A(f)}{\frac12k\cdot g(f)}=\frac{p_{w,in}^A(f)}{\frac12k}$$
And the effective temperature is $T_{eff,in}=T_S+T_A$

And thus $p_{w,out}^{tot}=\frac12k(T_A+T_S)\geq\frac12k\cdot T_0g_a(f)$. With $T_A\geq T_0=290[K]$ as a normal room temperature 

>[!rmk|*]
A 2-Port Network working under load matching and narrowband has $$SNR_{in}=SNR_{out}=\frac{P_v}{P_w}$$
>With the ratio of any 2 powers of the same kind (statistical, density, PSD, electrical)

>[!def] Noise Figure
>$$F(f)=\frac{p_{w,in}^{tot}}{p_{w,in}^S}=1+\frac{p_{w,in}^A}{p_{w,in}^S}$$

#### Cascade of Two-Port Network
>[!col]
>In a cascade of N two-port networks (in figure N=2) we can easily find the values of an equivalent single two-port network with the following formulas.
>
>![[Pasted image 20241030112126.png|Cascade of 2 Two-Port Network|350]]

$$\begin{align}
&g(f)=\prod_{i=1}^N g_i(f) \\
&T_A(f)=T_1(f)+\sum_{i=2}^N\frac{T_i(f)}{\prod_{j=1}^{N-1}g_j(f)}=T_1(f)+...+\frac{T_N(f)}{g_1(f)\cdot...\cdot g_{N-1}(f)} \\
&F(f)=F_1(f)+\sum_{i=2}^N\frac{F_i(f)-1}{\prod_{j=1}^{N-1}g_j(f)}=F_1(f)+...+\frac{F_N(f)-1}{g_1(f)\cdot...\cdot g_{N-1}(f)}
\end{align}$$
